    "q-335483": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335483",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+5x(t)+2y(t)+6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[-2,\\,0]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?",
      "worked_solution": "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-2,\\,y(t)=v(t),\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+5x(t)+2y(t)+6 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-2)^{2}+5(u(t)-2)+2v(t)+6 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-4u(t)+4)+5u(t)-10+2v(t)+6 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)+6 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)-2)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+6 \\\\  & =3u(t)-6-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+6 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
